Date: Thu, 28 Jan 93 11:15:41 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: PPL: Fraud

A good example from Hal Finney:

> Let's take an example case which would be dealt with today under fraud
> laws.  Suppose a food manufacturer lies about the ingredients in some
> food that he sells.  I buy hot dogs which claim to be fat free, and they
> are delicious, but it turns out that they are high in fat.
> 
> What is my recourse?  Can I sue for damages to my arteries and peace of mind?
> What kind of contractual relationship needs to exist between me and the
> manufacturer to encourage manufacturers to be honest on their labels.

* Option 1, aka "status quo." Hal finds a lawyer who'll file a class
action suit (anything less is probably not cost-effective for the
lawyer). Years go by. The arteries of the court system are clogged.
Eventually, Oscar Meyer makes a token settlement.

* Option 2, aka "PPL." Hal contacts Health Maintainers, Inc., his
chosen medical care/health maintenance organization. They shake their
heads, sigh, and say, "We urged you, Mr. Finney, to stay away from
_all_ hot dogs. Now, about your premiums..."

* Option 3, aka "reputations." Hal writes up his experiences and
advertises to the Net, perhaps for free, perhaps for some
remuneration. Others add their comments, and the reputation of Oscar
Meyer is tarnished (shades of the "Jack in the Box" situation).

* Option 4, aka "the total state." Advertising, which often involves
varying interpretations of the truth ("You _need_ this car!"), is
essentially banned except by authorization of the state, for
state-sanctioned and scientifically verified claims only. Hal, upon
finding fat in his hot dogs, attempts to sue the government. Hal fails.

Which world do we want to live in? Perhaps more to the point, which
world is the most reasonable (a loaded term, granted) world? (I'm
trying to take a Freidmanesque stance here, in terms of how a PPL
solution is the likeliest to ultimately emerge.)

I submit that some form of Option 2, PPL, mixed naturally with Option
3, reputations, is likeliest. It's how most of our
interactions--social, romantic, this list, etc.--are already run.

To see this, replace Hal's example as follows: 
      
"Suppose my girlfriend lied to me about how good she'd be for me. I
believed her and now we're married. What is my recourse? Can I sue for
damages to my peace of mind? What kind of contractual relationship
needs to exist between me and my wife to encourage us to be honest
about our promises?"

(apologies to Hal if it looks like I'm satirizing his argument--I'm
not.)

The point is this: many, many areas of life and commerce deal with
disappointments, unrealized expectations, disagreements, and
misunderstood promises. 

The _essential_ feature of markets which makes them work is *repeat
business* (and the related reputations). A store which gives you lousy
service doesn't have to be patronized again. A book which you thought
would be good and is not may cause you to avoid that author in the
future. A disappointing romantic relationship can be ended. And so on,
for lots of examples. (The usual anarchocapitalist stuff about
markets, tit-for-tat, reputations, defections, etc.)

Talk of lawsuits and legal redress is just the wrong way to go about
it. But if someone should really _want_ this kind of legal redress,
let them take out "marriage insurance" or somesuch. Let them pay hefty
premiums each year to pay for this kind of protection. Just don't ask
me to pay for services I don't want. (And don't ask me to cooperate
with "authorities" tracking down "promise breakers" unless I see it's
in my own interest to do so.)

Ditto for the food example. I _know_ hot dogs are filled with fat.
Some companies seem to have a better reputation than others. If Oscar
Meyer values its reputation, it will be careful to not lie. (BTW,
anonymous systems allow "rating" services to evolve without the usual
interference by the state and unhappy companies.)

Why should a well-known vegan on this list, Perry, pay to enforce "fat
in hot dog" claims when he never eats hot dogs? (Benson covers this
kind of issue well.)

(For those who doubt how private ratings services could work, consider
Underwriter's Laboratories (U.L.) and their ratings stickers. Those
who both eat hot dogs and are concerned about fat levels (the empty
set?), can choose products which have been evaluated by independent
testing labs. Details about forgeries of seals I'll leave as an
exercise for the reader.)

Live and learn. Once burned, shame on you. Twice burned, shame on me.
And so on...just basic wisdom of the ages.

