Date: Thu, 28 Jan 1993 07:30:47 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Cholesterol levels and meat eating

Dave Lubkin:
 
> I have seen claims that what happens is the high sugar diet leads to
> pocks in your blood vessels.  Your body uses cholesterol to salve the
> pocks.  If you keep your sugar intake high and lower your dietary fat,
> your cholesterol will still remain high.

This is an interesting theory, but refined sugar wouldn't be the
only culprit.  Unrefined sugars and honey are every bit as nasty
as refined sugars, and starchy foods get converted to sugar quickly
in the mouth and stomach. Two important numbers are the glycemic 
index, which predicts the rate of change in blood sugar, and the amount 
of carbohydrates, which will predict the total blood sugar dose.  The 
freindliest sugar is fructose, with a glycemic index of 20 compared to 
table sugar (sucrose, 59) and glucose (100).

The ultimate refined sugar, high-fructose corn syrup (manufactured
by a nifty cyborg catalyst I've described elsewhere), has replaced
most of the sucrose in pop.  Unfortuneately, since they don't post 
the percentage of fructose one still can't be confident of not getting
a high dose of sucrose from one's Pepsi.  Potatoes, bread, pasta,
etc. get converted to glucose, and all have a glycemic index similar 
or greater than table sugar.  Starches can often provide one with a nasty 
cycling through hyper- and hypoglycemia.  For more info see _The Life 
Extension Weight Loss Program_, Pearson & Shaw.  Carrots have a high
glycemic index (92) but are low in carbohydrates.  In ice cream, 
on the other hand, something in the cream binds the sucrose, giving a very 
low glycemic index (36), but alas the fat is nasty for other reasons.

When foods are combined, they often lower each other's glycemic
index.  It's a good idea to eat high diversity meals low in carbohydrates.
Usually my only high-carb meal is breakfast, where I try to get about half
from high-fiber cereal and half from fruit.

