Well, I wasn't looking at their explicit industrial policy, but their
policies that enable the consumer-goods delivery system to remain very
fragmented and inefficient, driving up the prices of consumer goods.
These inefficiencies seem to be due directly to government policy, and
the tight web of the Japanese distribution system.  In regards to
that, Japan has a very un-free market.  (Japan is only starting to get
"discount stores" of any sort.)  My suggestion is that this has caused
reduced consumption, leaving excess savings for investment.

