Date: Thu, 28 Jan 93 14:06:43 EST
From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: PPL: Fraud

> From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
> 
> Let's take an example case which would be dealt with today under fraud
> laws.  Suppose a food manufacturer lies about the ingredients in some
> food that he sells.  I buy hot dogs which claim to be fat free, and they
> are delicious, but it turns out that they are high in fat.
> 
> What is my recourse?  Can I sue for damages to my arteries and peace of mind?
> What kind of contractual relationship needs to exist between me and the
> manufacturer to encourage manufacturers to be honest on their labels.

It would seem that the manufacturer made a representation (which is that
the hotdogs were fat free), and that this representation was false. Given
this, he has breeched the contract. In breech of contract under the common
law, if I am not mistaken, you are liable not only for the difference between
what you gave the person and what they had, but between what you gave the
person and what you promised them. For example, if I claim that the antibiotics
I am giving you will cure a disease of yours, but I know that they are
just sugar pills, and you die, under the common law I am liable for your
death, as I claimed that what I was giving you was the cure. I owe the
difference between what was promised and what I did. Assuming that the
high fat hotdogs caused you some harm, you can sue for the damage that
they caused, given that you had been contractually promised something else.

Perry

