Date: Thu, 28 Jan 93 14:15:12 EST
From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: Fraud, Religion, etc.

> From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
> 
> ssandfort@attmail.com writes:
> >A person commits fraud if he KNOWINGLY (1) makes
> >MATERIAL MISREPRESENTATION (2) upon which another person RELIES
> >(3) to his DETRIMENT (4).  ...
> >
> >Could the same be said if the contract were for rain making?  The
> >draught has devastated Tim May's farm.  Slick Sandy shows up and
> >signs a contract with Tim promising to use psychic powers to make
> >it rain for US$100,000.  Tim puts the money in escrow.  Slick
> >Sandy dances in Tim's field.  If it rains, Sandy gets the money.
> >No questions asked.  If it doesn't rain, Sandy goes to the next
> >farm.  Maybe some hint of fraud there? Too obvious, you say.
> 
> This is a good example to focus on.  I do not want to sign up for a
> law that would allow itself to conclude fraud here because
> jurors/judge did not believe in psychic powers.  I don't want my law
> making those judgements.  Slim Tim the Corn King is stuck, I say.

I believe it is necessary at time for courts to make those sorts of judgements.
The issue is, I believe, a question of what is and is not fraud.

Now, if a person is not promising anything, i.e. the money is in escrow and
the rainmaker says "this will not necessarily work", I think that prehaps
no fraud or breech of contract has occured. 

On the other hand:
If the rain maker had signed a contract stating that a) he knew that he
could produce rain at will (material and knowing misrepresentation) b) signs
a contract promising that he will produce rain to fix Tim's crops (contractual
obligation), and c) the rain is not produced, under the common law as
I understand it, the rain maker is now liable not merely for the money Tim
paid, but for the difference between what was promised (rain) and what
was delivered (none), and in particular the rainmaker would be held
responsible for the costs associated with the continued failure of rain
to appear.

Perry

