Date: Thu, 28 Jan 93 16:07:28 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: TECH: Magneto-Optical Drive, a Review

Here are some comments on a new 128 MB magneto-optical drive I got for
my Macintoshes.

A few months back there was some discussion of storage options,
including "flopticals," Bernoulli cartridges, and MO drives. I ended
up getting the MO, for various reasons. Here are some comments, by
type.

* Floptical, from various manufacturers. 21 MB for drives cost of
about $400. Floptical cartridges look like floppies and cost about $20
each. Very slow, about 2x faster than a floppy (on the Mac at least).

(Many people on the Net, in comp.sys.mac.hardware, report being
generally unhappy with flopicals. Had they appeared on schedule, 2
years ago, they might've made it. Now they're too little, too late.)

* Syuest 44 MB and 88 MB. An old standard. Noisy, fast, and
unreliable. After my 44 MB had a major head crash, I resolved to avoid
them. Drive cost for a 44 MB is about $400, cartridges are $60. The
88s are only sligthtly more expensive.

(If you need compatibility with printing outfits, magazines, get a
Syquest. However, the standard may be shifting to the MOs.)

* Bernoulli, by Iomega. Very rugged, very fast. But nonstandard. Drive
cost is $500 for a 90 MB and $800 for a 150 MB. Cartridges are fairly
expensive, at about $120 for the 90s and $140 for the 150s.

(I almost got one of these, especially after seeing one at MacWorld
Expo being bounced around while reading a large graphics file.
However....)

* Magneto-optical drive, from about a dozen manufacturers. Several
mechanisms (Sony, Epson, Ricoh, MOST, Matsushita, etc.) are then
packaged by the drive makers. Very rugged (no pickup heads near the
disk surface), archival lifetime, and reasonably fast. Drive costs are
still high, at about $1200, but media costs are very low, at $40 for
each 129 MB cartridge (looks like a fat 3.5" floppy). The "crossover"
in costs comes at about 500-600 MB of total storage, with the MOs
thereafter cheaper to use than Bernoullis.

(I bought an APS drive for $1200 plus shipping. Check magazines for
ads.)

The MO is a joy to use. I archive about 2 MB/month of e-mail
(including the Extropians articles I want to save), so a single
cartridge will last about 5 years. (In theory. In practice, the files
are of course scattered around. And I now expect to save more stuff,
now that storage is so cheap.)

I hope this helps someone else out there.

-Tim May



