Date: Thu, 28 Jan 1993 16:57:17 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: PPL: Capriciousness?

Tim May:
> In a nutshell, I felt Harry Shapiro's "punishment" of
> Perry was unwarranted, given that we all make sweeping generalizations
> at times.

Even though this may reflect Harry's exact wording, I think the
precedent set was much more narrow.  I interpreted his judgement 
as punishing negative generalizations about list members on the 
list, not attacking generalization in general.  If that was
what Harry intended, I'd say that it was a good judgement (assuming
that the defendant had attacked the accuser publicly, which he didn't, 
and Harry revoked the punishment accordingly).

As an aside -- and I'm assuming the statute of limitations is over
on this case so that we can freely Monday-morning quarterback :-) -- if I 
were the defendant I might have filed three countercharges:

(a) engaging in personal attack against a list member on the list (by 
    filing the charge in public, instead of private);
(b) posting private e-mail on the list;
(c) filing deceptive charges (quoting private e-mail in a way that left 
    the reader assuming he was quoting list e-mail)

I'm not sure how many of these are punishable offenses in this
PPL, since most precedents have not yet been set, but what the heck, 
I'd hit him with both guns blazing.  In other words, putting myself 
in the defendant's shoes, I'd consider escalating the case to gain a 
reputation as a tough customer who should not be attacked lightly.  

N.B.: none of the above should be taken as a commentary on
the specific participants in this case!

On the other hand, such charges would make gratuitous use of Harry's
time, and I have no desire to alienate Harry or his friends on the
list.  This gets to Tim May's point about free goods.  Harry's
free justice service has been taken advantage of so sparingly because
list members are implicitly expected to be altruistic wrt this service, 
rather than selfishly wasting Harry's time.  This kind of reputation system
might only work in a tight e-mail community, and even then it breaks 
down for people who don't care much about their reputation in that 
community. 

> Mr. Singh has recently made more assertions
> about yogi levitation, unsupported by documentation of any kind other
> than citing mythology. Should he be charged? 

No, because this is not a negative generalization about a list
member.

> * a fee for bringing charges.

Transaction costs again rear their ugly head.  Could we rig up
a secure way to send Harry our credit card numbers?  (Can we
trust Harry with them? :-)

Of course, if the verdict goes my way I'll pay double.  ;-)

> * Token punishment.

I think that's what happened; a 1-day suspension from posting is
hardly serious.  (Well, maybe for a frequent poster like Perry it 
could be pretty serious :-)

Comments in general:

I suspect we might have a major problem arise in judging cases by
who is the good guy, who are our friends, who we agree with,
who we like and sympathize with, etc. rather than by the specific 
merits of the case.  The most likely way for a PPL in a small community 
to be capricious is by pretending to have a law, but then applying 
it with massive double standards because we bring these other 
factors into play.

Now, maybe we _want_ there to be double standards favoring our
friends.  Maybe we _don't_ want it, but it's inevitable anyway.
Either way, let's make it explicit; let's do our favor-swapping
and judge-bribing out in an open market, instead of pretending
we're being even-handed when we're not.  

N.B.: The above is again not a personal commentary on anybody at
all except me, rather it is intended as a comment on PPL!
IMHO, Harry's judgement was, despite these long odds,
remarkably even-handed (if a bit long-winded...oops, now I've 
done it... :-)

> (A sidenote: If _restitution_ is the goal of PPL, and not
> _punishment_, then why is our list set up the way it is? 

Now this is a good point.  But how can someone make restitution
for a public insult, with low transaction cost?

I suspect punishment will play a major role in PPL.  One of
the expectations we have is that people will be motivated
maintain their reputation.  This makes reputation a form of 
punishment; the criminal's loss of reputation doesn't do the 
victim any good.  From this, we might extract "punishment" as 
an abstract entity, a legitimate transaction that can extend 
beyond the loss of reputation to the loss of other things of value.
We can also derive punishment emerging out of reciprocal 
harm, or revenge, which must have evolved alongside reciprocal
altruism.

To end with a semi-random idea, how about a market in
reputation futures?  I'll bet $10 that .... aw, ferget it, 
it would be a personal comment...  :-)

